{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-138910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $7\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{2}-16t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-138910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-16t+5) \\\\  & =4t-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-16 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =16 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-138910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cec7fba097a02c39cb6919b642068593a7870c37d6aba2f00c5f23ee199f33b"} {"question" "q-138910" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-138910" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=2\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=4\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=6\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=7\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=8\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-138910" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=4\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-138913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $9\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=(t-4){e}^{-t}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-138913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-4){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}+(t-4)(-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(5-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-t){e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ 5-t & =0 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-138913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cec7fba097a02c39cb6919b642068593a7870c37d6aba2f00c5f23ee199f33b"} {"question" "q-138913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-138913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=10\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=3\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=4\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=5\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=8\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-138913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=5\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-345338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-345338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-345338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cec7fba097a02c39cb6919b642068593a7870c37d6aba2f00c5f23ee199f33b"} {"question" "q-345338" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-345338" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2(\\sec(t))^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-345340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-345340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-1}] \\\\  & =-{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-345340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cec7fba097a02c39cb6919b642068593a7870c37d6aba2f00c5f23ee199f33b"} {"question" "q-345340" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-345340" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{t}{(1+t^2)^2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-345390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=4{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+12t+24$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-345390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(4{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+12t+24) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-12t+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(12{t}^{2}-12t+12) \\\\  & =24t-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24t-12 & =0 \\\\ 24t & =12 \\\\ t & =\\frac{12}{24} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-345390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cec7fba097a02c39cb6919b642068593a7870c37d6aba2f00c5f23ee199f33b"} {"question" "q-345390" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"} {"question" "q-345390" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "c72645f217a739340dbe20f2eeeb5095919238f4404002e731f5ca5294b87677"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2333 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
